WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.373
Graph neural networks are famously easy to fool
with small structural changes,

00:00:04.453 --> 00:00:08.825
and the field has long treated that fragility as
pure adversarial vulnerability.

00:00:08.905 --> 00:00:13.278
This ICLR 2023 paper from the Technical University
of Munich asks a sharper question:

00:00:13.358 --> 00:00:17.731
do those small perturbations actually preserve a
node's true meaning?

00:00:17.811 --> 00:00:22.184
Using a semantics-aware notion of an adversarial graph,

00:00:22.264 --> 00:00:26.636
the authors show that for low-degree nodes many
"small" edge edits already change the ground-truth label,

00:00:26.716 --> 00:00:31.089
and that every graph neural network they test is
over-robust,

00:00:31.169 --> 00:00:35.542
staying stubbornly unchanged even after the
semantics have flipped.

00:00:35.622 --> 00:00:39.995
They then show that feeding the training graph's
label structure back in through label

00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:44.447
propagation sharply cuts this over-robustness while
improving accuracy and genuine adversarial

00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:48.900
robustness.
